   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 381 of the laws of 2012 amending the education law relating to the licensure of private proprietary schools, in relation to the effectiveness of certain provisions of such chapter",
    "basePrintNo": "S5037",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5037-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §12, Chap 381 of 2012",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5037\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 381 of the laws of 2012\namending the education law relating to the licensure of private\nproprietary schools, in relation to the effectiveness of certain\nprovisions of such chapter\n\nPURPOSE:  This bill would extend for an additional three years, the\nprovision that allows proprietary schools to operate under a candidacy\nstatus for a period of one year.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  The bill would amend section 12 of chapter 381\nof the laws of 2012 to extend for an additional three years the law\nthat allows proprietary schools to operate under a candidacy status\nfor a period of one year, with a possible six month extension, while\nits application for licensure is considered by the State Education\nDepartment (SED).\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  The Bureau of Proprietary School Supervision (BPSS)\noversees and monitors non-degree granting proprietary schools in New\nYork State.  The Bureau is responsible for ensuring that the overall\neducational quality of the programs offered will provide students with\nthe necessary skills to secure meaningful employment and for\nprotecting students' financial interests while attending proprietary\nschools.  Chapter 381 of the laws of 2012 allows proprietary schools\nto operate under a candidacy status for a period of one year while its\napplication for licensure is considered by SED. This one year\ncandidacy period allows the SED to full examine a proprietary school's\ncapacity to provide a quality education to its students and its\nability to comply with education law and the Commissioner's\nregulations before licensure. This bill would ensure that this process\nwould continue\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
